WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.781
Deserialization is object construction from data that may have crossed a trust boundary.

2
00:00:05.781 --> 00:00:12.021
The application receives text, bytes, or a structured document, and the serializer produces a .

3
00:00:12.021 --> 00:00:13.335
NET object.

4
00:00:13.335 --> 00:00:20.758
That object may now pass through services, validators, repositories, authorization checks, or business workflows.

5
00:00:20.758 --> 00:00:28.707
If the object can be created in an invalid or contradictory state, the problem moves from parsing into application logic.

6
00:00:28.707 --> 00:00:36.590
Constructors, setters, required members, nullable fields, default values, and initialization patterns all affect safety.

7
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:47.298
A DTO with nullable properties may be acceptable for reading partial data, but business code should not assume those values are present until validation proves it.

8
00:00:47.298 --> 00:00:51.108
A default value may make a missing field look intentional.

9
00:00:51.108 --> 00:00:57.611
A setter may allow state that the application would never create through its normal domain methods.

10
00:00:57.611 --> 00:01:03.523
Avoid designs where deserialized objects can quietly represent impossible business states.

11
00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:09.961
An ExampleOrderMessage should not be considered ready for processing merely because it was parsed.

12
00:01:09.961 --> 00:01:21.654
It should be checked for required identifiers, ownership context, supported status values, expected version, reasonable sizes, and any business conditions needed by the workflow.

13
00:01:21.654 --> 00:01:24.544
The result of validation should be explicit.

14
00:01:24.544 --> 00:01:28.749
Separate parsing, validation, authorization, and business logic.

15
00:01:28.749 --> 00:01:32.822
Parsing answers, can the data be read into the expected shape.

16
00:01:32.822 --> 00:01:37.486
Validation answers, does the data meet the contract and business rules.

17
00:01:37.486 --> 00:01:43.069
Authorization answers, is this actor allowed to perform this action on this resource.

18
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.945
Business logic answers, what should the application do now.

19
00:01:46.945 --> 00:01:53.186
Combining those concerns makes it harder to review failures and easier to trust data too early.

20
00:01:53.186 --> 00:01:59.295
Handle malformed, oversized, unexpected, incomplete, or incompatible serialized input safely.

21
00:01:59.295 --> 00:02:12.368
Defensive behavior might include rejecting the input, recording a safe diagnostic event, returning a limited error, moving a message to a review process, or preserving a failure state for operations.

22
00:02:12.368 --> 00:02:19.988
The exact behavior depends on the workflow, but it should not leak sensitive diagnostic detail to untrusted parties.

23
00:02:19.988 --> 00:02:26.031
Do not assume queues, caches, files, databases, or internal tools make serialized data safe.

24
00:02:26.031 --> 00:02:37.199
Those locations may contain old versions, imported data, corrupted records, administrative mistakes, replayed messages, or values produced by code that has since changed.

25
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:41.337
Validate when the data is used, not only when it first arrived.
